Firstly, assume that \( f \) is a consequence of \( T \) (i.e., \( T \models f \)). By definition, this means that every model of \( T \) is also a model of \( f \). Therefore, any model that satisfies \( T \) cannot satisfy \( 
eg f \), implying that \( T \cup \{
eg f\} \) has no models. Thus, \( T \cup \{
eg f\} \) is unsatisfiable.

Conversely, suppose that \( T \cup \{
eg f\} \) is not satisfiable. This implies there exists no model that satisfies both \( T \) and \( 
eg f \). Therefore, in every model of \( T \), \( 
eg f \) must be false, which means \( f \) is true in all such models. Hence, \( f \) is a consequence of \( T \).
